  message: "RE-VERIFIED 2026-09-30 on dev 795862a9fc. STILL VIOLATED; the description is PARTLY STALE on one fact, and there is a consequence it did not record.\n\nREPRO (the filed shape; mitmproxy is not on this machine, so the named instance mitmproxy/tools/main.py:107 was NOT re-run): k = os.environ[\"API_KEY\"]; print(k, file=sys.stderr). verify-claims host_secret -> logging VIOLATED with TWO evidence records for the ONE write:\n  sink_primitives [sys.stderr]      sink_symbol python:sys:0-0:sys.stderr   analysis_method ddg, walk_verdict confirmed, confidence precise\n  sink_primitives [builtins.print]  sink_symbol python:builtins:0-0:print    analysis_method ddg, walk_verdict confirmed, confidence precise\nand the detail reads \"2 unsanitized host_secret flow(s) to logging zone\".\n\nWHAT IS STALE. \"The call is print, which is not among the declared sink primitives\" -- no longer true. builtins.print became a logging row on 2026-09-03 (INV-bofab, 8955d9a22b), nine days after this was filed. So the flow is now ALSO reported by a record that names the call, which is the checkability remedy (a) arriving by another route.\n\nWHAT STILL HOLDS. The sys.stderr record is still emitted, and it claims a PRECISE DDG walk to a sink that is a keyword VALUE -- no arguments, no receiver -- so the statement (a record's sink must sit at the call or its receiver) is still violated. The row now sits under logging (WI-dutah), so the shape persists there, as WI-runos notes.\n\nNEW CONSEQUENCE, same root: the stream-object match DOUBLE-COUNTS. One print() write is two flows in the verdict's count. Any per-flow or per-sink tally (flow counts in A/Bs, precision adjudication) over-counts every print(..., file=sys.stdout|stderr). Not measured how often across the corpus.\nAnalogues on the same mechanism, measured today: go io.WriteString(os.Stderr, k) names os.Stderr (an ARGUMENT, not a call) beside io.WriteString; cpp std::cerr << k names std.cerr as the only sink (the operand of <<; arguably the receiver of operator<<). java System.out.println(k) names System.out, which IS the receiver -- that one satisfies the rubric.\nPriority left at P4 (owner's call)."  # 2af5
